{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_12","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"cd /home/nitro/Desktop/consensus-assurance/runs/2026-10-15_00-04-05-etcd_raft-real-run/draft && python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json, copy\nu = json.load(open('update.json'))\nold = copy.deepcopy(u)\n\nparent = None\nfor i in u['interpretations']:\n    if i['investigation_id'].startswith('ce33f7'):\n        parent = i\nassert parent is not None, 'parent interpretation not found'\n# drop the child interpretation: it is already recorded and unchanged\nu['interpretations'] = [parent]\n\nparent['source_ids'] = parent['source_ids'] + ['src_rawnode_step_test', 'src_nodestep_public_test']\n\nextra = (\\\" One review question this raises is whether the snapshot's own expectations contradict the reading, \\\"\n         \\\"and they do not: the filter's expectation table in util_test.go enumerates 23 message types with their \\\"\n         \\\"expected classification and omits MsgForgetLeader entirely, while the two tests that drive the inbound \\\"\n         \\\"path for every type - TestRawNodeStep on RawNode.Step and TestNodeStep on node.Step - derive their \\\"\n         \\\"assertion from IsLocalMsg itself, so a type absent from the table cannot be caught by them; the omission \\\"\n         \\\"is therefore invisible to the snapshot's own tests, which fits an oversight rather than a deliberate \\\"\n         \\\"classification, and it also means that classifying the type as local would contradict no recorded expectation.\\\")\nassert not parent['rationale'].endswith(extra)\nparent['rationale'] = parent['rationale'] + extra\n\nu['sources'] = [\n    {\\\"id\\\": \\\"src_rawnode_step_test\\\", \\\"file\\\": \\\"rawnode_test.go\\\", \\\"start_line\\\": 78, \\\"end_line\\\": 104, \\\"kind\\\": \\\"test_expectation\\\"},\n    {\\\"id\\\": \\\"src_nodestep_public_test\\\", \\\"file\\\": \\\"node_test.go\\\", \\\"start_line\\\": 51, \\\"end_line\\\": 84, \\\"kind\\\": \\\"test_expectation\\\"},\n]\n\nu['knowledge'] = [\n    {\n        \\\"kind\\\": \\\"fact\\\",\n        \\\"statement\\\": (\\\"The snapshot's own tests do not pin IsLocalMsg(MsgForgetLeader) in either direction, and its \\\"\n                      \\\"inbound-path tests cannot detect a type missing from the filter. TestIsLocalMsg (util_test.go) \\\"\n                      \\\"enumerates 23 message types with their expected classification - including the storage message \\\"\n                      \\\"types that come later in the enum - and omits MsgForgetLeader entirely. TestRawNodeStep steps a \\\"\n                      \\\"message of every type in MessageType_name through the real RawNode.Step and asserts \\\"\n                      \\\"ErrStepLocalMsg only when IsLocalMsg(msgt) is already true. TestNodeStep routes every type \\\"\n                      \\\"through the public node.Step and expects the receive channel to stay empty only when \\\"\n                      \\\"IsLocalMsg(msgt) is already true. Both tests read their expectation from the table under test, \\\"\n                      \\\"so neither would fail today for MsgForgetLeader, and neither would fail if the type were \\\"\n                      \\\"classified as local.\\\"),\n        \\\"conditions\\\": (\\\"Read from the captured snapshot as expectations, not executed in this run. RawNode.Step rejects \\\"\n                       \\\"a local-typed message with ErrStepLocalMsg when the sender is not a local thread id, whereas \\\"\n                       \\\"the public node.Step silently returns nil after ignoring it; the internal node.step path that \\\"\n                       \\\"node.ForgetLeader uses never passes through that check, which is why the two producers keep \\\"\n                       \\\"working either way.\\\"),\n        \\\"source_ids\\\": [\\\"src_islocal\\\", \\\"src_utiltest\\\", \\\"src_rawnode_step_test\\\", \\\"src_nodestep_public_test\\\"],\n    }\n]\n\nu['rationale'] = (\\\"With the boundary question closed, I spent the remaining budget on a source-only check of the one \\\"\n                  \\\"question a reviewer is most likely to ask next: whether the module's own expectations make the \\\"\n                  \\\"classification deliberate rather than an omission, and whether anything in the snapshot would \\\"\n                  \\\"notice the gap. Reading the test files answers both. TestIsLocalMsg enumerates 23 types with their \\\"\n                  \\\"expected local/network classification, including the storage message types that sit late in the \\\"\n                  \\\"enum, and never mentions MsgForgetLeader, so nothing pins its classification either way. The two \\\"\n                  \\\"tests that drive the inbound path for every message type - TestRawNodeStep on RawNode.Step and \\\"\n                  \\\"TestNodeStep on the public node.Step - both compute their expectation by calling IsLocalMsg on the \\\"\n                  \\\"very type under test, so a type absent from the table produces no assertion at all; the gap is \\\"\n                  \\\"structurally invisible to them, and classifying the type as local would break neither test. That \\\"\n                  \\\"does not by itself prove intent, so I recorded it as a fact in the index rather than inflating the \\\"\n                  \\\"finding: it tells a reviewer that the protection is untested for this type, and it removes the \\\"\n                  \\\"possible objection that a recorded expectation already endorses the current classification. The \\\"\n                  \\\"two test files are added as sources to the parent account with one sentence of context, since the \\\"\n                  \\\"account's defect claim rests on reading the absent entry as an omission.\\\") \n\njson.dump(u, open('update.json', 'w'), indent=1)\n\n# verification: only the intended changes\nassert set(old.keys()) == set(u.keys())\nassert old['sources'] \"'!= u['\"'sources']\nfor k in ('investigation_id', 'experiment_id', 'joint_history', 'executions', 'conclusion', 'defect_claim', 'counterevidence', 'limitations'):\n    assert old['interpretations'][1][k] == u['interpretations'][0][k], k\nassert u['interpretations'][0]['rationale'][:len(old['interpretations'][1]['rationale'])] == old['interpretations'][1]['rationale']\nassert u['interpretations'][0]['source_ids'][:-2] == old['interpretations'][1]['source_ids']\nprint('rewritten ok; parent rationale chars:', len(u['interpretations'][0]['rationale']))\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
